Interesting finding: `apps-srv/clients` returns **401 Unauthorized** (not 404). This means the endpoint exists and requires authentication. This is different from `/apps-srv/clients/register` which returns 404 (a different routing). The 401 confirms there's a client management API here.

Let me explore the apps-srv more and see if I can find valid client_ids from other endpoints. Also let me look at the `hornbachde-prod.mirakl.net` — this is the Mirakl marketplace backend which is a separate HORNBACH property (HORNBACH Marketplace is in scope as part of "company-owned infrastructure").

Let me also look into this more carefully.
[0m$ [0m# Let's examine the apps-srv/clients endpoint more. It returns 401, means it needs auth
# Let's see what auth it wants
echo "=== apps-srv/clients with various auth headers"
curl -s -X GET "https://auth.hornbach.com/apps-srv/clients" -H "Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4=" --max-time 10 2>/dev/null
echo ""
curl -s -X GET "https://auth.hornbach.com/apps-srv/clients" -H "Authorization: Bearer test" --max-time 10 2>/dev/null
echo ""
echo "=== apps-srv/clients with Accept: application/json"
curl -s -X GET "https://auth.hornbach.com/apps-srv/clients" -H "Accept: application/json" --max-time 10 2>/dev/null
echo ""
echo "=== check /users-srv/"
for p in "users" "users/search" "Clients" ""; do
  code=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" "https://auth.hornbach.com/users-srv/${p}" --max-time 5 2>/dev/null)
  echo "users-srv/${p} -> ${code}"
done
